{"title":"2018 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 20","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2018 AMC 10A #20</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_15\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2018 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 15\">2018 AMC 12A #15</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Very_Intuitive_and_Fast)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Very Intuitive and Fast)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Similar_to_Solution_2)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Similar to Solution 2)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Pi_Academy\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Pi Academy</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A scanning code consists of a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/4/5d4f93df0d5696a7a30826c6a7c604d1352c6198.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 \\times 7$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> grid of squares, with some of its squares colored black and the rest colored white. There must be at least one square of each color in this grid of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/c/34c8e6bf4d57cd6f2bb0d7d43e00f0f442f53b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$49$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> squares. A scanning code is called <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/3/fa3021e911d666c749c43cb1b5de961e0e303116.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textit{symmetric}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"16\" /> if its look does not change when the entire square is rotated by a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/d/f9d38eab3efc0dbd74471a71de651ff67c88fb63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90 ^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> counterclockwise around its center, nor when it is reflected across a line joining opposite corners or a line joining midpoints of opposite sides. What is the total number of possible symmetric scanning codes?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/3/0d319fa2e95be938d301fb7e08e715cf2bfe4b4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)} \\text{ 510} \\qquad \\textbf{(B)} \\text{ 1022} \\qquad \\textbf{(C)} \\text{ 8190} \\qquad \\textbf{(D)} \\text{ 8192} \\qquad \\textbf{(E)} \\text{ 65,534}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"474\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Draw a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/4/5d4f93df0d5696a7a30826c6a7c604d1352c6198.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 \\times 7$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> square.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/e/78ea46dd064f1505682597da32c8dcb3a351a9e6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|} \\hline K &amp; J &amp; H &amp; G &amp; H &amp; J &amp; K \\\\ \\hline J &amp; F &amp; E &amp; D &amp; E &amp; F &amp; J \\\\ \\hline H &amp; E &amp; C &amp; B &amp; C &amp; E &amp; H \\\\ \\hline G &amp; D &amp; B &amp; A &amp; B &amp; D &amp; G \\\\ \\hline H &amp; E &amp; C &amp; B &amp; C &amp; E &amp; H \\\\ \\hline J &amp; F &amp; E &amp; D &amp; E &amp; F &amp; J \\\\ \\hline K &amp; J &amp; H &amp; G &amp; H &amp; J &amp; K \\\\ \\hline \\end{tabular}\\]\" width=\"230\" height=\"145\" />\n</p><p>Start from the center and label all protruding cells symmetrically. (Note that \"I\" is left out of this labelling, so there are only 10 labels, not 11, as ending in K would suggest.)\n</p><p>More specifically, since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> given lines of symmetry (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> diagonals, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> vertical, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> horizontal) and they split the plot into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> equivalent sections, we can take just one-eighth and study it in particular. Each of these sections has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> distinct sub-squares, whether partially or in full. So since each can be colored either white or black, we choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/8/50811f350197d47b2ddb684f3b7d07ce96a0bdca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{10}=1024$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"83\" height=\"15\" /> but then subtract the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cases where all are white or all are black. That leaves us with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/4/55496b6c63d07400111f4577b899d4feeb05243f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\fbox{\\textbf{(B)} \\text{ 1022}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"84\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>There are only ten squares we get to actually choose, and two independent choices for each, for a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/8/cf856775e3de1168a9a4f29bc68d3651d0bf6051.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{10} = 1024$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"83\" height=\"15\" /> codes. Two codes must be subtracted (due to the rule that there must be at least one square of each color) for an answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/1/0713782428e94227d70dc64d113770680287f33b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\fbox{\\textbf{(B) }1022}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"79\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nNote that this problem is very similar to the 1996 AIME Problem 7 <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/1996_AIME_Problems/Problem_7\">https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/1996_AIME_Problems/Problem_7</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Very_Intuitive_and_Fast.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Very_Intuitive_and_Fast)\">Solution 2 (Very Intuitive and Fast)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Very Intuitive and Fast)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/0/d30c7253f3f1307b4835e9325013b29ef3cd4db1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(100pt); draw((0,0)--(8,0),linewidth(0.5)); draw((0,2)--(6,2),linewidth(0.5)); draw((0,4)--(4,4),linewidth(0.5)); draw((0,6)--(2,6),linewidth(0.5)); draw((2,6)--(2,0),linewidth(0.5)); draw((4,4)--(4,0),linewidth(0.5)); draw((6,2)--(6,0),linewidth(0.5)); draw((0,0)--(0,8),linewidth(0.5)); draw((0,8)--(8,0),dashed+linewidth(0.5)); [/asy]\" width=\"168\" height=\"168\" />\n</p><p>Imagine folding the scanning code along its lines of symmetry. There will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> regions which you have control over coloring. Since we must subtract off <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cases for the all-black and all-white cases, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/5/725f99eaea80983b28986b53a1eb793c4b763698.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{10}-2=\\boxed{\\textbf{(B) } 1022.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"166\" height=\"28\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/8/30813847c1a6a391c8c6368465c8a8337f0cb9e0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|} \\hline T &amp; T &amp; T &amp; X &amp; T &amp; T &amp; T \\\\ \\hline T &amp; T &amp; T &amp; Y&amp; T &amp; T &amp; T \\\\ \\hline T &amp; T &amp; T &amp; Z &amp; T &amp; T &amp; T \\\\ \\hline X &amp; Y &amp; Z &amp; W &amp; Z &amp; Y &amp; X \\\\ \\hline T &amp; T &amp; T &amp; Z &amp; T &amp; T &amp; T \\\\ \\hline T &amp; T &amp; T &amp; Y &amp; T &amp; T &amp; T \\\\ \\hline T &amp; T &amp; T &amp; X &amp; T &amp; T &amp; T \\\\ \\hline \\end{tabular}\\]\" width=\"231\" height=\"145\" />\n</p><p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d278803d72fcc493afb1559174a483aa7f41d143.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> squares in the corners of this <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/4/5d4f93df0d5696a7a30826c6a7c604d1352c6198.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 \\times 7$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> square, and there is a horizontal and vertical stripe through the middle. Because we need to have symmetry when the diagonals and midpoints of the large square is connected, we can create a table like this: (Different letters represent different color choices between black and white)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/8/4485cb12038ad89a92620c16ef5cb752449440d0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|} \\hline A &amp; B &amp; C \\\\ \\hline B &amp; D &amp; E \\\\ \\hline C &amp; E &amp; F \\\\ \\hline \\end{tabular}\\]\" width=\"98\" height=\"63\" />\n(Note that coloring one <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d278803d72fcc493afb1559174a483aa7f41d143.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> square will also determine the colorings of the other <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> because the large <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/4/5d4f93df0d5696a7a30826c6a7c604d1352c6198.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 \\times 7$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> square must look the same when it is rotated by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/e/22ec0d2e4202839322d26a23dcb13f79821df5d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" />)\n</p><p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> different letters and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> choices of color (black and white) for each letter, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/3/c23f7dacd533c95a26bd926214b42d6cc00fb29f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^6=64$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"59\" height=\"15\" /> colorings of a proper <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d278803d72fcc493afb1559174a483aa7f41d143.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> square. Now, all that's left are the horizontal and vertical stripes. Using similar logic, we can see that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> different letters, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/8/9584bdb500d49089d884caad5e9f4484b6267bbf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^4=16$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"58\" height=\"15\" /> different colorings. Multiplying them together gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/6/0f656921ab7904a3829d77eccc955f816ddd2da4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$16 \\times 64 = 1024$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"119\" height=\"13\" />. Going back to the question, we see that \"there must be at least one square of each color in this grid of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/c/34c8e6bf4d57cd6f2bb0d7d43e00f0f442f53b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$49$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> squares.\" We must then eliminate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> options: an all-black grid and an all-white grid. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/9/e79f33cbbc9e1282512dabbddfb603cf540e1f20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1024-2= \\boxed{\\textbf{(B)} 1022} \\\\ \\phantom{}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 10px\" width=\"168\" height=\"28\" /> \n-hansenhe\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Similar_to_Solution_2.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Similar_to_Solution_2)\">Solution 4 (Similar to Solution 2)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Similar to Solution 2)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A symmetric code following the second and third conditions (diagonal and straight lines of symmetry) will automatically fulfill the first one. We can work with the grid by splitting it into different categories: the center (C), the diagonals across the corners (D), the straight lines across the midpoints (S), and the remaining cubes (R) as shown in the diagram below.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/0/9600ed1319aa7445e89f37af2be2b54ff6a1ba5e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|} \\hline D &amp; R &amp; R &amp; S &amp; R &amp; R &amp; D \\\\ \\hline R &amp; D &amp; R &amp; S &amp; R &amp; D &amp; R \\\\ \\hline R &amp; R &amp; D &amp; S &amp; D &amp; R &amp; R \\\\ \\hline S &amp; S &amp; S &amp; C &amp; S &amp; S &amp; S \\\\ \\hline R &amp; R &amp; D &amp; S &amp; D &amp; R &amp; R \\\\ \\hline R &amp; D &amp; R &amp; S &amp; R &amp; D &amp; R \\\\ \\hline D &amp; R &amp; R &amp; S &amp; R &amp; R &amp; D \\\\ \\hline \\end{tabular}\\]\" width=\"228\" height=\"145\" />\n</p><p>If we account for symmetry, we will only need to pay attention to a small section:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/1/5a1447a668d9ab02c74454ddcfc192b414dc8a00.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|} \\hline D &amp;   &amp;   &amp;   \\\\ \\hline R &amp; D &amp;   &amp;   \\\\ \\hline R &amp; R &amp; D &amp;   \\\\ \\hline S &amp; S &amp; S &amp; C \\\\ \\hline \\end{tabular}\\]\" width=\"131\" height=\"83\" /> \n</p><p>Note: this shows part of the full grid\n</p><p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/6/3f6a4ab443c94f13ae3ad0efbc8c6fa07b58a930.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^3$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" /> states of D, R, and S, and there are 2 states of C so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/e/6becbd6af17e4f999a1d31e4ac7971a5c84a4c57.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^3 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"100\" height=\"17\" /> and subtracting 2 cases for all white and all black will bring <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/6/5561dbbc404a07538db258037d066a1381aae772.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{10} - 2 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(B)} 1022}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"157\" height=\"30\" /> as our answer.\n</p><p>~Polyline\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Pi_Academy\">Video Solution by Pi Academy</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Pi Academy\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/lDk1--vGCYc?si=lPYpCtBdfuec72Xm\">https://youtu.be/lDk1--vGCYc?si=lPYpCtBdfuec72Xm</a>\n</p><p>~ Pi Academy\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2\">Video Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/M22S82Am2zM\">https://youtu.be/M22S82Am2zM</a>\n</p><p>~IceMatrix\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span 